## Supporting Multiple Tables

The outbox pattern works best when all events go into one standard table.

For example:

```text
OUTBOX_EVENT
    ORDER_CREATED
    ORDER_CANCELLED
    CUSTOMER_CREATED
    PAYMENT_RECEIVED
    INVOICE_GENERATED
```

Then each event type has a handler:

```text
ORDER_CREATED       -> OrderCreatedHandler
CUSTOMER_CREATED    -> CustomerCreatedHandler
PAYMENT_RECEIVED    -> PaymentReceivedHandler
```

This is cleaner than writing different pollers for every business table.

---

## Supporting Multiple Oracle Databases

If you have multiple Oracle databases, create an `OUTBOX_EVENT` table in each database.

Then your processor can be extended like this:

```yaml
outbox:
  databases:
    customerDb:
      jdbc-url: jdbc:oracle:thin:@//customer-host:1521/CUSTOMERPDB
      username: customer_user
      password: customer_password

    orderDb:
      jdbc-url: jdbc:oracle:thin:@//order-host:1521/ORDERPDB
      username: order_user
      password: order_password
```

Architecture:

```text
Spring Boot Outbox Processor
    |
    |-- Poll customerDb.OUTBOX_EVENT
    |-- Poll orderDb.OUTBOX_EVENT
    |-- Dispatch events to handlers
```

You can reuse the multi-database structure from the previous answer, but poll `OUTBOX_EVENT` instead of many business tables.

